# LINE ENDINGS ARE PART OF THE BUILD HERE, not a style preference.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its SYSTEM gitconfig, so every
# Windows clone of this repo used to check out CRLF while the index stayed LF.
# `docs/tracker-md.js` splits on '\n', and in JavaScript `\r` is a line
# terminator — `.` does not match it and `$` does not sit before it — so
# FIELD_RE matched NOTHING and the whole tracker toolchain went quietly wrong:
#
#   npm run status   printed 0/13 design fields for all 17 bosses AND "DONE"
#                    on five of them, and reported zero outstanding design work
#                    while 165 fields were actually [wip]
#   npm run sync     blanked attackName/weaponName/weaponClass on all 17 bosses
#                    in design/boss-data.json, then printed a success line
#   npm test         three failures in tests/tracker.test.js
#
# None of it was visible in CI, which runs on Ubuntu and therefore always had LF.
#
# This file makes the working tree match the index everywhere. The index is
# already LF, so adding it produces no commit noise — it only changes what a
# checkout writes to disk.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two exceptions, both because something other than git reads them.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git never touches the bytes. The keystore especially: it is the
# project's one stable signing identity, and a single mangled byte would mean
# every phone with the game installed has to uninstall first and lose its save.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.keystore binary
*.jar binary
*.apk binary
